### 1. Overall Sentiment

The dominant sentiment in early **2026** is **cautiously optimistic but noticeably more anxious and fragmented** than in the pre-2022 “boom” era. In professional circles, Software Engineering is still widely viewed as a resilient, high-leverage career—but with a stronger undercurrent of **job-market uncertainty**, **constant re-skilling pressure**, and **questions about what “being an engineer” means when AI writes a lot of the first draft**.

A clear pattern in professional discourse is **pragmatic adoption rather than uncritical enthusiasm**: AI tools are now broadly used, but sentiment has cooled as day-to-day friction (trust, correctness, debugging burden) becomes more salient. For example, large developer surveys show **very high usage** of AI tools while **positive sentiment toward them has declined** compared to prior years, and frustrations with “almost right” output are common. ([Stack Overflow][1])

In public commentary, the tone is more volatile and headline-driven: replacement narratives (“AI will do the job”) are louder, while practitioner voices more often emphasize **shift and augmentation** over immediate disappearance. That gap—between public “replacement” framing and professional “workflow transformation” framing—is one reason overall sentiment feels **split**.

